where \eqn{V_u} is \code{EM3.cov.res$Vu} and \eqn{w_i} is the
corresponding element of \code{EM3.cov.res$weights}.

The covariance between the \eqn{i}-th and \eqn{j}-th random effects is

\deqn{
\sigma_{ij}
=
\rho_{ij}\sigma_i\sigma_j
=
\rho_{ij}\sqrt{\sigma_i^2\sigma_j^2}.
}

Define

\deqn{
A_i = Z_iK_iZ_i'
}

and

\deqn{
C_{ij}
=
Z_iK_{ij}Z_j'
+
Z_jK_{ji}Z_i'.
}

Then,

\deqn{
V
=
\sum_i \sigma_i^2 A_i
+
\sum_{i<j}
\rho_{ij}
\sqrt{\sigma_i^2\sigma_j^2}
C_{ij}
+
\sigma_e^2I.
}

The derivatives are

\deqn{
\frac{\partial V}{\partial\sigma_i^2}
=
A_i
+
\frac{1}{2}
\sum_{j \ne i}
\rho_{ij}
\sqrt{\frac{\sigma_j^2}{\sigma_i^2}}
C_{ij},
}

\deqn{
\frac{\partial V}{\partial\rho_{ij}}
=
\sqrt{\sigma_i^2\sigma_j^2}C_{ij},
}

and

\deqn{
\frac{\partial V}{\partial\sigma_e^2}=I.
}

For REML, the expected Fisher information matrix is calculated as

\deqn{
I_{ab}
=
\frac{1}{2}
\mathrm{tr}(PV_aPV_b),
}

where

\deqn{
P =
V^{-1}
-
V^{-1}X
(X'V^{-1}X)^{-1}
X'V^{-1}.
}

For ML, \eqn{P} is replaced with \eqn{V^{-1}}.
